    {"source": ["s017"], "source_head": "s017", "target": [], "target_head": null, "status": "unrepresented", "confidence": "medium", "note": "Rule 14 aligns the waw where the ASV writes a connective for it; every circumstantial waw so aligned in this chapter takes a clause-initial subordinator (v. 29 \"when\"). Here the ASV subordinates with the participle \"being\", which rule 20 assigns to the predicate, leaving the waw no word of its own, as at 1KI.19.19. Aligning it to \"yet\" instead remains defensible."},
    {"source": ["s018"], "source_head": "s018", "target": ["t018"], "target_head": "t018", "status": "aligned", "confidence": "high", "note": ""},
    {"source": ["s019"], "source_head": "s019", "target": ["t019", "t021", "t023"], "target_head": "t023", "status": "aligned", "confidence": "high", "note": "Verbless clause: the supplied copula \"being\" joins the predicate it licenses, and \"a\" belongs to the noun."},
    {"source": [], "source_head": null, "target": ["t020"], "target_head": "t020", "status": "added", "confidence": "medium", "note": "This \"yet\" is temporal \"still\", not the adversative connective rule 14 would put on the waw, and it stands inside the clause after the copula rather than at its edge. The sense is עוֹד's elsewhere (1KI.1.14, 1KI.1.22) and no such morpheme is present, so nothing licenses it; the waw's circumstantial force is already spent on \"being\" under rule 20. Reading it as the waw's rendering remains defensible."},
